  "worked_solution": "In this situation, we want to find $\\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t}$ at the moment when $r=10,$ and we are also told that  $\\frac{\\text{d}C}{\\text{d}t}=4\\pi .$\n\nTo start, let's set up an equation relating the area $A$ and the circumference $C$ of the circle. Since $C=2\\pi r$ and $A=\\pi {r}^{2},$ the relationship between $A$ and $C$ is\n\n$$\nA=\\pi {r}^{2}=\\pi {(\\frac{C}{2\\pi })}^{2}=\\frac{{C}^{2}}{4\\pi }.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating this relationship with respect to $t$ using implicit differentiation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(A) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{{C}^{2}}{4\\pi }) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}C}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}C}(\\frac{{C}^{2}}{4\\pi }) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}C}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot \\frac{C}{2\\pi }.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWhen $r=10\\,\\text{mm},$ $C=2\\pi (10)=20\\pi \\text{\u00a0mm}.$ Hence, substituting $\\frac{\\text{d}C}{\\text{d}t}=4\\pi $ and $C=20\\pi $, and solving for $\\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t}.$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t} & =4\\pi \\cdot \\frac{20\\pi }{2\\pi } \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t} & =40\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe conclude that the area is increasing at a rate of $40\\pi {\\text{\u00a0mm}}^{2}\\text{/s}.$",
